        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Vibration levels are a critical concern for the performance of material science experiments requiring a microgravity environment such as crystal growth and fluid dynamics.  Exercise is required on Shuttle missions to maintain crewmember health but has been shown to degrade the microgravity environment due to the vibration induced by exercising crewmembers.  During the STS-50 mission, the Extended Duration Orbiter (EDO) Cycle Ergometer was shown to produce lower levels of vibration as compared with the Shuttle treadmill.  Three different cycle mounting configurations were investigated during the mission.  The Ergometer Vibration Isolation System (EVIS) and the bungee restraint system both reduced the level of vibration from cycle exercise down to the background level.  In addition, the cycle was hard-mounted to the flight deck for four sessions; this configuration resulted in significantly higher levels of vibration than the EVIS or bungee system.  A test was conducted during the mission using the EVIS in a passive mode which showed that a passive isolation system can attenuate cycle exercise vibrations to the background level.  The Passive Cycle Isolation System (PCIS) is intended to preserve the microgravity environment by attenuating cycling forces before they are transmitted to the Shuttle and to Shuttle payloads.  This DTO tested the vibration isolation capability of the PCIS and determined the impact of the PCIS on Shuttle operations.  The SAMS hardware was used to measure the levels of vibration.
<p>
The objectives of this study were:<br>
1. To identify the magnitude and frequency of the vibration on the Orbiter and/or Spacelab Module generated by cycle exercise while using the PCIS.  Compare ergometer vibration data using the PCIS and a hard-mounted cycle on the same flight provided it does not interfere with microgravity experiments.<br>
2. Evaluate the ease of set up and stowage of the PCIS.  Measure the time and resources required to install and stow the PCIS on-orbit.<br>
3. Evaluate the impact of the PCIS on the performance of exercise activities.<br> 
4. Evaluate the impact of the PCIS on crew habitation in the middeck area.<br>
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Documentary 35mm still photos of exercising crewmembers and of the complete system (configured for exercise and on-orbit stowage) were requested.  Video footage included the set up of the isolation system, during some part of the exercise session, and stowage of the system while on-orbit and prior to landing.  The scenes required during the exercise session included:  cycle/rider movements during exercise, movements of interface fixture relative to the middeck and LIOH door movements.  Live video downlink of any of the above scenes was highly desirable.
<p>
Three familiarization sessions for the passive cycle isolation system were performed preflight.  These sessions included instructions on setting up the interface fixture, ergometer, and the PCIS.  The crew rehearsed the procedures for using the cycle ergometer and PCIS including attaching the four isolators to the middeck, moving the interface fixture from the launch to on-orbit position, setting up the ergometer on the interface fixture, on-orbit stowage of the ergometer and PCIS, and stowage of the entire system for landing.
<p>	
Prior to the first exercise session in-flight, the four isolators were attached to the middeck after which the interface fixture was attached to the isolators.  The ergometer was then installed on the interface fixture.  The sensor head and interface bracket was attached to the ergometer.  The ergometer and PCIS remained installed throughout the flight or stowed in one of several different configurations at the crew's discretion.  At least one exercise session (provided microgravity constraints were not violated) was requested with the cycle hard-mounted to the middeck using the mounting feet integral to the interface fixture. This session was required to provide baseline acceleration data.  This DTO was accomplished by crewmembers doing any exercise protocol on the cycle ergometer or by participants of other exercise DSOs (i.e., 618).  Prior to landing, the entire system was stowed in the same configuration used during launch.
<p>	
Crewmembers recorded comments on ease of system set-up and stowage, stability and motion of the system while exercising, and perceived effort during the performance of exercise activities.  Acceleration data was recorded on the Orbiter (middeck and/or Spacelab module and/or payload bay) for some period before and after each exercise session as well as during the session.  Acceleration data was also taken with the interface fixture hard-mounted to the middeck to provide baseline data to be used in assessing the PCIS performance.  Crewmembers performed the same exercise protocol when acquiring data using the hard-mounted system versus the PCIS.  Following the final exercise session the four isolators were restowed in the locker and the ergometer and interface fixture were stowed for landing.
<p>
